  "query_preview": "(This evening through Friday)...\nIssued at 152 PM EDT Fri Sep 4 2026\n\nBroad ridging centered over the Plains will amplify while slowly \ndrifting westward. This allows a trough over the northeastern US to \npush into the Ohio River Valley, bringing an end to the current heat \nwave. The ridge, temporarily shunted west, returns next week with \nrenewed heat and humidity. The secondary round of heat does not \nappear to be as prolonged, with another trough arriving late next \nweek along with a reduction in temperatures. Scattered showers and \nthunderstorms are possible with both troughs, though chances are \ngenerally 40 percent or lower due to limited moisture and forcing.\n\nToday through tonight\n\nTemperatures are already into the mid 90s as of 1pm. Highs are once \nagain expected to reach the mid to upper 90s with dew points in the \nlow 70s. A Heat Advisory remains in effect as Heat Risk remains in \nthe extreme category with heat indices around 105 degrees.\n\nA few isolated showers and thunderstorms are possible today but \noverall the chances are low. Limited large-scale forcing and dryness \nin the atmosphere will make cumulus growth difficult. ACARS \nsoundings show a very dry atmosphere along with a cap around 700mb. \nAny attempts at convective initiation will be confined to subtle \nfeatures like gravity waves or horizontal convective rolls. Overall, \nconvective chances are very low today.\n\nSaturday\n\nThe first of the aforementioned cold fronts arrives on Saturday from \nthe northeast. Atmospheric moisture is modeled to increase tomorrow \nwith better lapse rates. With the front acting as an initiating \nforce, our convective chances are a lot better compared to today. \nStill, large-scale mid/upper-level forcing is displaced to the \nnortheast. Our convective chances are therefore dependent on the \nfront itself.\n\nTwo scenarios exist. First, the front initiates convection late \ntonight or tomorrow morning, which then propagates southwestward \ninto the southwesterly low-level flow ahead of the boundary. This \nwould generally limit severe potential with the primary hazard \nbeing heavy rainfall and localized flooding.\n\nSecond, the front enters central Indiana dry allowing daytime \nheating to maximize. Increased instability along the front would \nhelp initiate convection during the afternoon, which would propagate \nsoutheastward in line with the shear vector. Bulk shear is not \nexcessive, but enough to allow for some storm-scale organization \ninto clusters or small bowing segments. Steeper lapse rates and \nresidual dryness may present a downburst or wind threat.\n\nIn the second scenario, maximized heating may necessitate the need \nto expand the Heat Advisory. High-resolution guidance showing \nscenario two also shows high temperatures once again in the mid 90s. \nThe only exception are locations to our northeast behind the front, \nwith FROPA some time around noon at the IN/OH line.\n\nSUNDAY AND MONDAY\n\nNortheasterly flow behind the cold front brings a cool and dry air \nmass into the region. Temperatures fall back to near normal (low 80s \nfor highs and low 60s for lows) along with much lower dew points. \nThese conditions continue into early next week before the ridge \nbegins building back in by late Tuesday into Wednesday.",
